<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silvio Pellico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silvio Pellico was an Italian writer and patriot from Saluzzo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1820 for his links with the Carbonari, he spent ten years in Austrian prisons, mostly in the Spielberg fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>. His memoir of imprisonment, Le mie prigioni (1832), was read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and damaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silvio-pellico.jpg" alt="Silvio Pellico" width="222" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silvio_Pellico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
